## The coast before the resorts.

Most guests on Egypt's southern Red Sea coast see the reef, the pool and the airport. Few realise that an hour up the coast is a port that was trading with the Nile valley long before the resorts, or that a short drive south of Marsa Alam is a national park where the desert runs straight down to mangroves and empty bays. This day is for the curious ones: a fort, a town with a long memory, and a valley so quiet it feels like the edge of the world.

## El Quseir, where the desert road began

For centuries, El Quseir was the place where the Nile valley met the sea. Caravans crossed the desert from the river; pilgrims sailed for Mecca; spices and coffee came the other way. The Ottomans raised a fort here in the sixteenth century to guard it all. In 1799 Napoleon's army took it and rebuilt it, and British ships bombarded it from the sea. Walk its walls, then the lanes of the old town, where merchants' houses still lean their wooden balconies over the street.

> Every ship that sailed from here carried someone setting out on the journey of their life.

## Wadi el-Gemal, the valley of the camels

In the afternoon the car turns south, past Marsa Alam, into Wadi el-Gemal National Park. Protected since 2003 and home to the Ababda, it is a landscape of wide valleys, lone acacias and mountains that go pink as the sun drops, running down to mangroves and a coast of offshore islands. You stop somewhere silent, and your concierge makes tea in the desert before the light goes and the car takes you home.

## Arranged so you only have to look

Your concierge takes care of it all, with the local knowledge and craft that make a long day feel easy: every ticket and fee, the drive, and the table for lunch. You simply step into the car after breakfast and out of it, back at your hotel, with a day nobody else on your sun lounger row has had.
